Specifically, layax reimplements or adapts the following from Laya:
  - the typed-decision question schema (choice / score / noul) and option rendering
  - the strictly-proper-scoring-rule reward (log + spherical + RPS), in layax/losses.py
  - the per-(question type, option count) temperature bucketing and clamp behaviour
  - the ECE binning, so reported calibration numbers are comparable

Model weights

The published layax checkpoints are derivative works of the Laya model
convaiinnovations/laya (Apache License 2.0), which is itself built on
answerdotai/ModernBERT-large (Apache License 2.0). The weights were modified by
fine-tuning the encoder and adding new late-interaction projection layers. The
checkpoints are distributed under the Apache License 2.0.

Training and evaluation data

The published checkpoints were trained and evaluated on the datasets below. The
data was modified: it was split into train, competence, calibration and test
subsets, and label names were rendered as option text (underscores and colons
replaced with spaces). No dataset is redistributed with layax.

  - Banking77, PolyAI, CC BY 4.0.
    Casanueva et al. 2020, "Efficient Intent Detection with Dual Sentence Encoders".
    Loaded from the mteb/banking77 mirror. That mirror carries an MIT tag, which
    should not be relied on. The original dataset license is CC BY 4.0.
  - CLINC150 (clinc/clinc_oos, "plus" configuration), CC BY 3.0.
    Larson et al. 2019, "An Evaluation Dataset for Intent Classification and
    Out-of-Scope Prediction".
  - MASSIVE (AmazonScience/massive, en-US locale), Amazon, CC BY 4.0.
    FitzGerald et al. 2022, "MASSIVE: A 1M-Example Multilingual Natural Language
    Understanding Dataset with 51 Typologically-Diverse Languages".
